---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-jpeg-to-100kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={100} fixedFormat="jpeg" inputLabel="Drop the JPG files you need at 100 KB" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Which encoder does the work</h2>
    <p>
      This page uses <strong>MozJPEG</strong>, compiled to WebAssembly and running in your browser.
      Professional image pipelines use the same encoder. It differs from the encoder built into your
      browser in ways that matter for a fixed budget: trellis quantisation chooses coefficients that
      cost fewer bits for the same visual result, progressive scan scripts are optimised per image, and
      the Huffman tables are built for each image. The result is 5–15% smaller at the same apparent
      quality, which at a 100 KB limit is the difference between quality 68 and quality 76.
    </p>

    <h2>Generation loss: the one rule that matters</h2>
    <p>
      JPEG splits the image into 8 × 8 blocks, converts each to frequency coefficients and rounds them.
      Each decode and re-encode rounds coefficients that were already rounded, so the errors add up:
      edges get halos, flat areas get blotches, and colour bleeds across block boundaries. The same photo
      saved ten times at quality 80 looks much worse than the original saved once at quality 40.
    </p>
    <p>
      Always start from the highest-quality copy you have, ideally directly from the camera or the
      phone. Never put a compressed result through a second pass. If the output is not good enough,
      change the crop or the target.
    </p>

    <h2>How the target is hit</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Encode at quality 92 and measure. If it fits, that is your file.</li>
      <li>If not, do a binary search of the quality range: encode at the midpoint, keep it and raise the floor if it fits, lower the ceiling if it does not.</li>
      <li>After nine or ten rounds the range is one quality point wide, and the best encode that fits wins.</li>
      <li>If even quality 5 is too large, search the pixel dimensions with a Lanczos3 resample.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The result card lists each step with its byte count, so you can see how fast size falls with
      quality for your photo. This tells you more than a generic quality table.
    </p>

    <h2>Why two JPEGs at 100 KB can look completely different</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Subject</th><th>How it compresses</th><th>Quality typically reached at 100 KB</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Studio portrait, plain backdrop</td><td>Very well: large smooth areas</td><td>85–92</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Product on white</td><td>Very well</td><td>85–92</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Street scene</td><td>Moderately</td><td>65–78</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trees, grass, water</td><td>Badly: high-frequency detail everywhere</td><td>45–60</td></tr>
          <tr><td>High-ISO night shot</td><td>Worst case: noise cannot be compressed</td><td>35–50</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If your photo is in the bottom rows, the fix is fewer pixels, not a better encoder. Crop tighter or
      limit the width, and the same 100 KB gives a much higher quality setting.
    </p>

    <h2>Chroma subsampling, briefly</h2>
    <p>
      JPEG stores brightness at full resolution and colour at half, because the eye is much more
      sensitive to brightness. For this reason, red text on a dark background looks fringed in a JPEG,
      and screenshots belong in PNG. A JPEG screenshot converted to PNG later is not repaired: the colour
      detail was discarded at the first save.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything runs on your machine</h2>
    <p>
      The tool runs ten to eighteen full encodes of a large photo in less than a second, with no upload,
      so an exact byte target is practical. A server-based compressor needs a round trip per attempt.
      Your photo, including its EXIF and GPS data, stays in this tab, and the metadata is dropped when
      the tool re-encodes from pixels.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb">Compress any image to 100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-png-to-100kb">PNG at 100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/jpg-to-webp">JPG to WebP</a> for a smaller file at the same quality ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
